Chapter: CapNetworkDenied

 Runtime probe: the four network helpers consult the per-process
 capability word before driving the NE2000. The program declares
 Network.Read, so the boot grant derived from its own manifest
 includes cap-network-read and the first status read succeeds. It
 then strips its own grant word in the process table and asks again.
 The kernel refuses with -1 without touching the device.

 Before 2026-07-13 there was nothing to refuse with: the bits were
 defined, granted, and read by nobody, so a stripped grant changed
 nothing and the NIC answered anyway.

Section: Body

  proc0-cap-addr : Integer = 20536

  strip-grant : Integer -> Integer
  strip-grant (x) =
    let lo = poke-32 proc0-cap-addr 0 0
    in let hi = poke-32 proc0-cap-addr 4 0
    in 0

  opening : [Console, Network.Read] Nothing = act
    before <- net-status
    print-line-uni ("granted: " & show before)
    print-line-uni ("stripped: " & show (strip-grant 0))
    after <- net-status
    print-line-uni ("denied: " & show after)
  end
